parts=['''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>D4D review follow-up — evidence and robustness</title><style>
body{margin:0;background:#eef3f6;color:#203044;font:17px/1.6 system-ui,sans-serif}main{max-width:1200px;margin:auto;padding:42px 48px;background:white}h1{font-size:39px;line-height:1.2}h2{font-size:27px;margin-top:45px}h3{font-size:21px}a{color:#087b8f}p{max-width:1080px}.small,figcaption{font-size:14px;color:#526277}.callout{background:#fff4de;border-left:5px solid #c59435;padding:17px 22px}.cards{display:flex;gap:18px;flex-wrap:wrap}.card{padding:17px 22px;border-radius:8px;background:#edf5f7}.card b{display:block;font-size:30px;color:#13798e}figure{margin:32px 0}figure img{width:100%;border:1px solid #e0e6ec}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:14px;margin:20px 0}td,th{padding:10px;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid #d9e2e8}th{background:#eaf2f6}code{background:#eef2f6;padding:1px 4px;font-size:.9em}details{padding:15px 20px;border:1px solid #d7e1e8;margin:20px 0}summary{font-weight:650;cursor:pointer}blockquote{border-left:3px solid #c9dce4;padding-left:18px;font-size:15px}pre{white-space:pre-wrap;word-break:break-word;font-size:13px;max-height:500px;overflow:auto}nav a{margin-right:18px}input{width:94%;padding:12px;font-size:16px;border:1px solid #9fadb8}.case{border-top:2px solid #cddde5;padding-top:20px;margin-top:30px}.pill{display:inline-block;padding:3px 9px;background:#e6f1f5;border-radius:5px;font-size:14px}.matrix{overflow:auto}@media print{main{padding:0}body{background:white}figure,.case{break-inside:avoid}}
</style><main><p class="small">FOLLOW-UP ANALYSIS · SEPTEMBER 30, 2026 · ORIGINAL SCORES PRESERVED</p><h1>Separate missing information,<br>source limitations, and reviewer behavior</h1><div class="callout" id="historical-cohort" data-affected-records="CM4AI_v8_rep2 CM4AI_v8_rep3"><p><strong>Historical cohort: September 2026.</strong> These reports describe frozen generic-v7/v8 records and their September 12 CBORG reference rescore, not the current generator or a current validation of its outputs. Saved render metadata dates v7 to September 1–2 and v8 to September 5 (VOICE replicate 1) or September 7 (the other 11 records). Labels beginning <code>2026-09-04f/g</code> are run identifiers, not the generation dates of all v8 records. Provenance recording timestamps are distinct from exact model-call start times; see the saved <a href="https://github.com/bridge2ai/data-sheets-schema/blob/d2e8b1d3f2cae162c5fb9e3c31c63c9864baf205/data/d4d_concatenated/claudecode_api_core/2026-09-04f_claude-opus-5-api-generic-v8_rep1/VOICE_provenance.yaml">VOICE v8 replicate 1</a> and <a href="https://github.com/bridge2ai/data-sheets-schema/blob/d2e8b1d3f2cae162c5fb9e3c31c63c9864baf205/data/d4d_concatenated/claudecode_api_core/2026-09-04g_claude-opus-5-api-generic-v8_rep2/CM4AI_provenance.yaml">CM4AI v8 replicate 2</a> records.</p><p><strong>CM4AI generation-regime caveat.</strong> V8 replicates 2 and 3 returned no thinking block in the full, reconciliation, report, and report re-check phases. The historical requests omitted the <code>thinking</code> parameter; the <a href="https://github.com/bridge2ai/data-sheets-schema/blob/d2e8b1d3f2cae162c5fb9e3c31c63c9864baf205/notes/generic_v8_analysis_plan.md#L351-L365">generation analysis documents an input-correlated adaptive outcome</a>, not an explicit request to disable thinking or an established proxy fault. Both <a href="https://github.com/bridge2ai/data-sheets-schema/blob/d2e8b1d3f2cae162c5fb9e3c31c63c9864baf205/data/d4d_concatenated/claudecode_api_core/2026-09-04g_claude-opus-5-api-generic-v8_rep2/CM4AI_provenance.yaml">replicate 2</a> and <a href="https://github.com/bridge2ai/data-sheets-schema/blob/d2e8b1d3f2cae162c5fb9e3c31c63c9864baf205/data/d4d_concatenated/claudecode_api_core/2026-09-04g_claude-opus-5-api-generic-v8_rep3/CM4AI_provenance.yaml">replicate 3</a> remain included in every original score and pooled result. The pooled CM4AI comparison therefore mixes observed thinking regimes. This limits attribution of differences to the v8 package alone; it does not establish that the regime explains any score difference or justify a corrected score.</p></div><p>The follow-up adds 10 figures, structural-presence checks, review-overlap measures, uniform item-removal sensitivity analyses, and an evidence assessment of 32 stratified comments. The main change is interpretive: reviewer labels are useful signals, but they are not a direct census of missing fields or verified factual errors.</p><nav><a href="#findings">Findings</a><a href="#missing">Missingness</a><a href="#agreement">Agreement</a><a href="#sensitivity">Robustness</a><a href="#evidence">Evidence review</a><a href="#sources">Sources</a><a href="#methods">Methods</a></nav><div class="cards"><div class="card"><b>242 / 384</b>unpopulated selected record–field pairs</div><div class="card"><b>144 / 242</b>named by completeness comments</div><div class="card"><b>32</b>comments reviewed against evidence</div><div class="card"><b>4 / 4</b>version directions survive Q19 removal</div></div><div class="callout"><strong>Evidence-review boundary.</strong> The 32-comment assessment is an assistant review of frozen D4Ds and selected historical source excerpts. It is neither a human-validated gold standard nor an estimate of error prevalence in all 562 comments. Compound comments can contain both supported and overstated claims. No original D4D, evaluation score, or evaluator text was changed.</div><p><a href="D4D_review_summary.html">Original report and exploratory tests</a> · <a href="followup_plan.txt">Implementation plan</a> · <a href="D4D_review_complete_bundle.zip">Complete downloadable bundle</a></p><h2 id="findings">What changes—and what remains stable</h2>
<ul><li><strong>The earlier missing-field figure was a review-conditioned view.</strong> All 24 records omit top-level download_url, imputation_protocols, parent_datasets, and resources; only 18, 13, 12, and 9 records respectively receive completeness comments naming those fields. Structural absence is not automatically an error or an applicable requirement.</li><li><strong>Stable scores conceal substantial variation in commentary.</strong> Repeated rubric10 scores differ by only 0–2 percentage points, while mean pairwise overlap of flagged fields is 54–73%. Requiring the review family to match lowers overlap to 28–47%.</li><li><strong>Version directions survive the disputed-item sensitivity.</strong> With Q19 omitted uniformly, AI_READI remains slightly lower in v8, CHORUS higher, and CM4AI and VOICE lower. Removing Q13 as well does not change those directions. These are diagnostic score subsets, not corrected scores or stronger causal evidence.</li><li><strong>Several sampled allegations need reinterpretation.</strong> The DOI-format inconsistency in S30 is contradicted by the historical schema; S12 overlooks an explicit software/data license distinction; S22 compares local version numbers across separate deposits; S24 treats the source’s V2/2.0 display conventions as a defect.</li><li><strong>Some supported concerns remain actionable.</strong> Release-specific temporal coverage, processing-software provenance, component repository identifiers, and demographic detail remain incomplete in selected D4Ds. S32 exposes adult/pediatric scope ambiguity in generated wording. The review does not establish that all these gaps were fillable from the supplied sources.</li></ul>''']
caps={
'15_structural_absence':('Structural absence versus completeness comments','The field list is the 16 fields selected in the initial report, not the full schema. An empty/missing root value is absent; false and zero are populated. “Not mentioned” means not named by a native completeness comment—it may appear under another review type. No applicability, requiredness, source availability, or reviewer-recall rate is inferred.'),
'16_agreement':('Do reviews flag the same fields?','Jaccard = shared flagged-set size / union size. Type-aware overlap uses (review family, field), pooling correctness and content_accuracy into one family. There is no assumed universe of negatives. Cross-rubric comparisons cover six records per dataset; repeats are three pairwise comparisons among three ratings of one v7 rep1 record per dataset. Those pairs are dependent and are not used for significance tests.'),
'17_score_sensitivity':('Uniform item-removal sensitivity','Each diagnostic removes both earned points and the applicable maximum for the omitted items, uniformly across all records. No replacement item score is assigned. The original denominator exclusions remain in force. Similar directions do not remove execution confounding or adjudicate any other rating.'),
'18_denominator_effect':('Fixed versus adjusted denominators','Fixed percentages divide by the rubric’s full maximum; adjusted percentages divide by the applicable maximum. The plot shows the mean difference. Report the basis whenever comparing datasets, particularly CM4AI. Neither score is silently substituted for the other.'),
'19_signature_counts':('Conservative grouping of repeated field topics','Strict grouping merges comments only within the same record when their native type and complete root-field set match. Broad grouping pools the two accuracy types and removes source_caveats, notes, and description as generic context roots unless nothing else remains. This is transparent grouping, not semantic identification of unique real-world defects. Different concerns can share a signature and the same concern can have different signatures.'),
'20_item_distributions':('Item-level score distributions','Full, partial, zero, and not-applicable counts sum to 24 per question. These distributions expose ceiling effects and applicability decisions hidden by means. Q19 and selected Q13 judgments retain the original audit qualifications.'),
'21_source_heterogeneity':('Source associations by dataset','These are the same within-record, within-section contrasts calculated in the original follow-up statistics: no new hypothesis tests. Positive means a higher proportion of reviewer-flagged roots among source-linked fields. The source type, section, and underlying dataset remain entangled; N/A indicates no eligible comparison. The underlying per-section denominator table is linked below.'),
'22_sample_dispositions':('Reclassification of the balanced sample','Eight sampled comments per broad native family, balanced across four datasets and two rubrics. One primary disposition summarizes each comment; explanations preserve mixed components. None of these counts should be projected to the complete corpus or interpreted as reviewer precision.'),
'23_recurring_topics':('Recurring review topics, with within-record duplication removed','Eight selected regex-based topic rules applied to issue descriptions. A record is counted once per topic across both rubrics. Topics overlap, may co-occur in a single comment, and do not exhaustively partition the corpus. Rules and every issue-to-topic assignment are exported for inspection.'),
'24_path_specificity':('How much of the source association is only a broad-field join?','Each candidate (issue, mentioned field, source type) counted here already shares a populated root with a coverage receipt. Paths normalize array indices away. Same nested path, ancestor relationship, and sibling/other paths are distinguished from root-only comments. Even identical normalized paths do not verify that the cited passage supports the specific claim; counts are not independent observations.')}
