paths=get('path_linkage_sensitivity');exact=sum(x['exact_nested_match'] for x in paths);root=sum(x['root_only_comment'] for x in paths)
parts.append(f'<p>Of {len(paths):,} candidate source-to-comment links sharing a populated root, {root:,} involve comments naming only the root. Only {exact:,} also have an identical normalized nested path in a receipt. This is a linkage-specificity diagnostic, not an error-source attribution rate. Array indices are removed, so even the “same nested path” category can refer to different list entries. A future claim-level analysis would need exact entry identities and entailment review.</p><p><a href="data/source_section_contrasts.json">Source-section comparisons and denominators</a> · <a href="data/followup/path_linkage_sensitivity.json">Candidate links</a> · <a href="data/followup/path_linkage_summary.json">Path-match summary</a></p>')
parts.append('''<h2 id="methods">Methods, validation, and presentation guidance</h2><p>All 56 input references were rechecked against the stored SHA-256 hashes, covering 24 distinct D4Ds. The 48 primary ratings remain the basis of cross-rubric and version summaries; eight extra rubric10 ratings are used only for repeat agreement. Score-subset arithmetic was checked against the original totals. Historical evidence bundles were recovered using the pipeline’s run_chunks helper, which verifies the generation-time bundle/chunk identity. Historical schema descriptions were read from the registered instrument commit rather than inferred from current main.</p><p>Evidence assessments are intentionally narrow: source differences do not prove a generated falsehood; structural absence does not prove applicability; a source’s explicit “no” is meaningful content; and reviewer disagreement does not establish which judgment is correct. No internet lookups, new evaluator model calls, legal/compliance determinations, or changes to the source repository were made.</p><p><strong>Suggested main presentation:</strong> use structural absence (15), review agreement (16), the disposition matrix (22), and version sensitivity (17) as the four new main figures. Use recurring topics (23) and source heterogeneity (21) for the narrative, with the remaining figures in supplementary material.</p><p><strong>Suggested claim:</strong> “Structural completeness and semantic review findings measured different aspects of documentation. Repeated evaluation produced stable aggregate scores but less stable field-level findings. Several sampled correctness/consistency comments reflected source limitations, source conventions, or reviewer overinterpretation rather than demonstrated generated inaccuracies. Dataset-specific version directions persisted under uniform removal of disputed provenance items, without resolving the study’s execution and adjudication limitations.”</p><p><a href="data/followup/checks.json">Arithmetic and input checks</a> · <a href="data/followup/sample_selection.json">Sample selection</a> · <a href="data/followup/sample_assessments.json">Case assessments</a> · <a href="followup_scripts/analyze_followup.py">Analysis/figure script</a> · <a href="followup_scripts/adjudicate_sample.py">Assessment definitions</a> · <a href="followup_README.txt">Reproduction instructions</a></p><script>document.getElementById('casefilter').addEventListener('input',function(){let n=0,q=this.value.toLowerCase();document.querySelectorAll('.case').forEach(c=>{let ok=c.textContent.toLowerCase().includes(q);c.hidden=!ok;if(ok)n++});document.getElementById('casecount').textContent=n+' cases';});</script></main></html>''')
